        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ado" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Azure DevOps Boards&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:#5c5c5c&quot;&gt;Accepted backlog, evidence links&lt;/span&gt;" style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;verticalAlign=top;arcSize=12;fillColor=#f7f6f4;strokeColor=#8a8781;dashed=1;dashPattern=5 4;spacingTop=37;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1460" y="960" width="200" height="80" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ado-icon" parent="ado" value="" style="shape=image;html=1;aspect=fixed;imageAspect=0;image=data:image/svg+xml,;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="88.0" y="11" width="24" height="24" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="wiki" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Wiki / meta-repo&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:#5c5c5c&quot;&gt;Architecture + decisions&lt;/span&gt;" style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;verticalAlign=top;arcSize=12;fillColor=#f7f6f4;strokeColor=#8a8781;dashed=1;dashPattern=5 4;spacingTop=37;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="620" y="1380" width="200" height="80" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="wiki-icon" parent="wiki" value="" style="shape=image;html=1;aspect=fixed;imageAspect=0;image=data:image/svg+xml,;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="88.0" y="11" width="24" height="24" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-approval" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Approval and publish: what they check and why&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;What is approved:&lt;/b&gt; one exact version of the draft: the decision item in the SharePoint list holds the document version label, its content hash, the approver identities and the model and prompt versions that produced it.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why a version and a hash:&lt;/b&gt; SharePoint has no transaction across edit and approval, so an edit after approval would otherwise publish unreviewed text. The version label and hash tie the approval to exact content.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;What the publisher checks:&lt;/b&gt; it re-reads the decision, recomputes the hash, confirms both a Product Owner and a Software Architect approver by Entra group, and that the requester is not an approver.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why a queue:&lt;/b&gt; an LLM call and a work item write can fail or time out. The queue gives retries and a dead-letter queue, and the publisher stores the correlation ID on the work item so a retry never creates a second one.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why two functions:&lt;/b&gt; the analyser cannot write to Azure DevOps and the publisher cannot call the model, so a prompt injection in a request cannot reach the backlog.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Alerts:&lt;/b&gt; dead-letter depth above 0, failed runs, and drafts waiting for review longer than the SLA.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Does not prove:&lt;/b&gt; that the draft is right. Reviewers still decide, so the review app shows the diff and sources to keep approval from becoming a rubber stamp." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="1520" width="800" height="275" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-plain" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;How a request becomes a backlog item&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;What happens&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;• Someone raises a request in ServiceNow, or fills the small-change form, which creates the same ticket&lt;br&gt;• The system checks what is missing, writes a story with acceptance criteria and drafts a solution view from the product&amp;#x27;s own architecture notes&lt;br&gt;• The Product Owner and a Software Architect read the draft next to the original, then clarify, redirect, reject or accept it&lt;br&gt;• Only an accepted version becomes a work item in Azure DevOps, with links back to the evidence&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why we do it this way&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;• People stay in charge: nothing reaches the backlog without both approvals, and rejected requests keep their decision record&lt;br&gt;• Each product&amp;#x27;s notes stay with that product, so one team never sees another&amp;#x27;s&lt;br&gt;• Urgent incidents keep the existing ServiceNow route and SLA; they never wait for AI&lt;br&gt;• Existing delivery gates still decide what ships: this only gets requests into the backlog" style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="860" y="1520" width="800" height="275" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-mind" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;What to keep in mind&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Other teams:&lt;/b&gt; ServiceNow needs an integration user and an outbound connection. Azure DevOps needs the publisher&amp;#x27;s managed identity added to the organization and project. The wiki and meta-repo owners must allow the indexer.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Power Platform is not Terraform:&lt;/b&gt; flows and apps ship as solutions through their own pipeline. The Azure side (functions, queues, search, model, monitoring) fits the golden-template shape.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Starting requirements (business case):&lt;/b&gt; pilot Product Owner and an Engineering owner, Power Platform environment and licences, a SharePoint site and library, Foundry model access and budget, scoped ServiceNow and Azure DevOps integration identities, and agreed security and data boundaries.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Current blockers:&lt;/b&gt; the ServiceNow production service account does not yet have the ITIL role, so Change Request, Task, Request, Certification Renewal and similar flows can only be tested against UAT data. Incident, polling and small-change flows are ready to deploy. The Power Platform credit and licensing questionnaire is still pending, so the licence cost is unknown.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Licences:&lt;/b&gt; Power Apps, Power Automate and any premium connectors are paid per user or per flow and are not in the Azure bill.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Data:&lt;/b&gt; request text may be sensitive. The model deployment fixes a data zone, prompts are not logged, and SharePoint and the queues follow the retention policy.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Zones:&lt;/b&gt; uat and prod use zone-redundant Service Bus (Premium), search replicas and zone-redundant functions; dev and qa do not. SharePoint, Power Platform and Foundry availability follow the Microsoft service.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Phasing:&lt;/b&gt; now the intake pilot; next, accepted specs feed coding and review agents; later, change records, evidence packs and ServiceNow status reconciliation. The dashed status edge belongs to that last phase.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Cost:&lt;/b&gt; not yet estimated, and the business case says the capacity benefit is not yet measured. Service Bus tier, AI Search and model tokens drive the Azure side. Price it from the Azure Retail Prices API before this becomes a template.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Not built yet:&lt;/b&gt; this is a proposal. No Terraform, pipelines or tests exist for it, and nothing has been planned or applied." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="1825" width="800" height="415" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-why" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Why pick this design, and its trade-offs&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;SharePoint, not Dataverse:&lt;/b&gt; the business case already lists a SharePoint site and library, and one product with one team stays far below the list limits. Trade-off: no transactions or row security, so the design leans on version labels, hashes and a site per product. Dataverse is the upgrade when intake scales toward about 200 engineers.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Queues, not direct HTTP from the flow:&lt;/b&gt; a model call outlasts the 120 s HTTP limit and needs retries and a dead-letter queue. Trade-off: one more resource to run and monitor.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Two functions with separate identities, not one:&lt;/b&gt; least privilege: read and draft versus write to Azure DevOps. Trade-off: two deployments and two sets of role assignments.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;AI Search, not scanning the wiki per request:&lt;/b&gt; per-product security trimming, citations and predictable latency. Trade-off: an index to keep fresh and pay for; skip it if the context is small enough to put in the prompt.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Private endpoints, not public endpoints with keys:&lt;/b&gt; requests may hold sensitive text, so the Azure side has no public path. Trade-off: DNS and VNet work, and Power Platform must reach the functions through an approved gateway or connector.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Thin Power Automate, not logic in flows:&lt;/b&gt; flows are hard to test and review, so they only move data. Trade-off: logic lives in two places, flows and functions.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Service Bus, not Storage Queue:&lt;/b&gt; native dead-letter queues and zone redundancy. Trade-off: costs more; Storage Queue is enough for a one-team MVP.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Intake form creates the ServiceNow ticket:&lt;/b&gt; one record and one SLA for every request. Trade-off: the form depends on the ServiceNow integration being up." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="860" y="1825" width="800" height="415" />
        </mxCell>
